{%- comment -%}
What the search page downloads. Jekyll rebuilds it from the archive on every
build, so the nightly sync cannot leave the two out of step, and nothing derived
has to be committed.

Only the fields the search itself needs are here rather than the whole archive,
which is roughly three megabytes of media urls, reaction breakdowns and profile
pictures that a keyword search has no use for. The keys are single letters for
the same reason: at thirteen hundred posts the names would cost more than the
values.

  d  the day it was posted
  t  the text, which is both what is matched and what the snippet is cut from
  u  the permalink, only when it is one a browser should follow, with LinkedIn's
     share tracking trimmed off the end: it is a sixth of the whole download and
     the post opens the same without it
  a  the original author, only on reposts, since own posts are the default
  x  set when the archive only holds a preview of the post

The scheme check on u mirrors the one in linkedin-post-cards.html, so the page
can attach a href without re-examining scraped data.
{%- endcomment -%}
[
{%- for post in site.data.linkedin-posts.data.posts -%}
{%- unless forloop.first %},{% endunless %}
{%- assign author_name = post.author.first_name | append: " " | append: post.author.last_name | strip -%}
{%- assign url_scheme = post.url | slice: 0, 8 -%}
{%- assign post_url = post.url | split: "?" | first -%}
{"d":{{ post.posted_at.date | date: "%Y-%m-%d" | jsonify }},"t":{{ post.text | jsonify }}
{%- if url_scheme == "https://" %},"u":{{ post_url | jsonify }}{% endif %}
{%- if post.author.username != "williammilisic" and author_name != "" %},"a":{{ author_name | jsonify }}{% endif %}
{%- if post.text_truncated %},"x":1{% endif %}}
{%- endfor %}
]
